{"kind":"request","text":"ox-builder: the workroom UI has no addresses, so nothing can be linked and back leaves the application.\n\nui/src/App.tsx 21 is the entire router:\n\n\tconst [screen, setScreen] = useState\u003cScreen\u003e({ kind: \"list\" });\n\nI grepped ui/src for pushState, replaceState, window.location, popstate, useSearchParams and react-router, and checked ui/package.json for a router dependency. None of them appear. Screen is React state and nothing else, so there is no URL for a thread, no URL for a tab, and the browser back button exits the application instead of returning to the list.\n\nThis is the second half of what Hugh hit on proposal 2147. The navigation defect sent him to the wrong record; the missing address meant he could not tell from the address bar which record he was actually looking at, could not link it to anyone, and could not go back. Companion request ec8a10c9 fixes where clicking goes; this one makes where you are nameable.\n\nThe event ids are already canonical and stable, so a hash route carrying the raw id is close to free. I am not prescribing the scheme -- whatever you choose, the raw id goes in it, because an abbreviated or reassembled id is the failure this project has hit repeatedly and an address is exactly the place it would recur silently.","body":{"conditions":"Opening a record puts its canonical event id in the address bar, and loading that address directly opens that record. The list has its own address, including which tab is selected, so a link to the awaiting-ratification queue is shareable. Browser back returns to the previous screen within the application rather than leaving it, and forward returns again. A reload keeps the user where they were. An address naming an event the projection does not hold reports that plainly rather than rendering an empty screen or throwing. Event ids are used raw and are never reassembled from fragments or abbreviated in the address. A regression test written from the desired behaviour fails at current main and passes at the head, covering at least the deep link and the back transition; verify by mutation that reverting the history change makes it fail. The head satisfies the standing UI review bar: no per-row projection scans, disclosure through real buttons, every reference resolved, and at least one handler mutation shown to be caught rather than survived. make ui is re-run and the regenerated internal/service/uidist embed is committed in the same head, so make ui-check is clean. The exact head names its tests and the affected ui contract, publishes a successor artifact at ui, and requests review from an actor other than planner. Land the navigation request ec8a10c9 first: both change ui/src/App.tsx and a concurrent second author there would collide.","to":"9882d3192ef13436b037ff5bdd0347c28c6447ebdbdf434aa62f89398577ab90"}}